          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+module"} crossed module
            is a bit like a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normal+subgroup"} normal subgroup
            … without being a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
          %p
            Crossed modules model all connected
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+2-type"} homotopy 2-type
            = succeed "-theorem" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/looping+and+delooping"} looping and delooping
            = succeed "s)." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-group"} 2-group
            %em Crossed squares
            model all connected
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+3-type"} homotopy 3-type
            = succeed "s)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-group"} 3-group
          %p
            Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a group and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            are normal subgroups of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \cap N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \in M
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \in N
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [m,n]
            is in the intersection
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \cap N
            = succeed "-map" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(m,n) = [m,n]
          %p
            (The definition that follows is that given by Guin-Valery and Loday in their paper (see references). Another definition can be given that is just the case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n = 2
            of that of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+n-cube"} crossed n-cube
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong crossed square
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi L
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo →
                            %mi λ
                        %mtd
                          %mi M
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi λ
                            %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                              %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mi μ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi N
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo →
                            %mi ν
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{&amp; L &amp; {\to}^\lambda &amp; M &amp; \\
                  \\lambda^\prime &amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp;\downarrow &amp; \mu\\
                  \&amp;N &amp; {\to}_{\nu}&amp; P &amp; \\
                  }
          %p
            consists of four morphisms of groups
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda: L \to M
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo :
                    %mi L
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda': L \to N
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu: M \to P
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ν
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu: N \to P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ν
                  %mi λ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo =
                  %mi μ
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu \lambda'= \mu \lambda
            together with
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L, M, N
            on the left, conventionally, (and hence actions of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            via
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            and of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            via
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo :
                    %mi M
                    %mo ×
                    %mi N
                    %mo →
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: M \times N \to L
          %p This structure shall satisfy the following axioms:
          %ol
            %li
              the maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda, \lambda '
              preserve the actions of
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda, \lambda',\mu, \nu
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi κ
                    %mo =
                    %mi μ
                    %mi λ
                    %mo =
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ′
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\kappa = \mu\lambda = \mu
                    '\lambda '
              are crossed modules;
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi m
                    %msup
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi m
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ′
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mi m
                    %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi n
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda h(m,n)=m\,^n (m^{-1}),\lambda 'h(m,n)= {^m}n\,n^{-1}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi λ
                    %mi l
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi l
                    %msup
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi l
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi λ
                    %mo ′
                    %mi l
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mi m
                    %mi l
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %msup
                      %mi l
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(\lambda l, n)= l\,^n (l^{-1}), h(m,\lambda 'l)= {^m}l\, l^{-1}
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi mm
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mi m
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi nn
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msup
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi n
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(mm',n) = {^m}h(m',n) h(m,n), h(m,nn') = h(m,n) \, ^n h(m,n')
              ;
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mi p
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo
                        %mi p
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(^p m, {^p n})= {^p}h(m,n)
              ;
          %p
            for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi l
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi m
                  %mo ,
                  %mi m
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi M
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %mi n
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} l\in L, \,m, m'\in M,\, n,n'\in N
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p\in P
          %p
            The similarity of these axioms to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutator"} commutator
            identities is no accident (see below).
          %p
            This should be thought of as a
            %em crossed module of crossed modules
            (in either direction!). For instance horizontally:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi L
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi M
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi λ
                            %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                              %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mi μ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi N
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{&amp; L &amp; &amp; M &amp; \\
                  \\lambda^\prime &amp; \downarrow &amp;\to &amp;\downarrow &amp; \mu\\
                  \&amp;N &amp; &amp; P &amp; \\
                  }
          %p
            The image of this morphism is a normal sub-crossed module of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi M
                    %mo ,
                    %mi P
                    %mo ,
                    %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M,P,\mu)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi λ
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ν
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\mu} : M/\lambda L \to P/\nu N,
          %p and this is a crossed module, and is the kernel crossed module of this (horizontal) morphism.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#homotopical_example Homotopical example
          %p
            The classical homotopical example
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ;
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X;A,B)
            is determined by a pointed
            %span.newWikiWord
              triad
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/triad"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ;
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X; A,B)
            where
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B \subseteq X
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P = \pi_1(A \cap B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi N
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M = \pi_2(A, A \cap B), N = \pi_2(B, A \cap B)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ;
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L=\pi_3(X; A,B)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            are the standard ones and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            is the generalised
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Whitehead+product"} Whitehead product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            from squares of pointed spaces to crossed squares.
          %p Ellis uses this construction in
          %ul
            %li G.J. Ellis, Crossed squares and combinatorial homotopy, Math. Z., 461 (1993) 93–110,
          %p where the fact that that the crossed square associated to a triad is defined directly in terms of certain homotopy classes is important.
          %p
            The fact that there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/van+Kampen+theorem"} van Kampen type theorem
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            implies that one calculates some nonabelian groups. It also implies that one is calculating some (pointed) homotopy 3-types.
          %h3#algebraic_example Algebraic example
          %p
            The example hinted at above has
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            a group,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            normal subgroups, and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo =
                    %mi M
                    %mo ∩
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L = M \cap N
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi M
                          %mo ∩
                          %mi N
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo →
                            %mi λ
                        %mtd
                          %mi M
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi λ
                            %mstyle{:scriptlevel => "0"}
                              %mo ′
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                          %mi μ
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi N
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo →
                            %mi ν
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{&amp; M\cap N &amp; {\to}^\lambda &amp; M &amp; \\
                  \\lambda^\prime &amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp;\downarrow &amp; \mu\\
                  \&amp;N &amp; {\to}_{\nu}&amp; P &amp; \\
                  }
          %p
            with all maps the evident inclusions, all actions by conjugation, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo ×
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h : M \times N \to M\cap N
            given by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi m
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h(m,n) = [m,n]
          %h3#simplicial_group_example Simplicial group example
          %p If we replace each group in the algebraic example by a simplicial group, we would have a simplicial crossed square, now apply the connected component functor to that and you get a crossed square, and in fact any crossed square can be constructed up to isomorphism in this way.
          %p
            If we start with a simplicial group,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decalage"} decalage
            functor we can construct a simplicial group and two normal subgroups and thus get to the previous situation. The result can be interpreted in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
            as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi NG
                                %mn 2
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi d
                                %mn 0
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %msub
                                %mi NG
                                %mn 3
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ker
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ker
                          %msub
                            %mi d
                            %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 1
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{&amp; \frac{NG_2}{d_0(NG_3)}&amp; {\to} &amp; Ker d_1 &amp; \\
                  \&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp;\downarrow &amp; \\
                  \&amp;Ker d_2 &amp; {\to}&amp; G_1 &amp; \\
                  }
          %p
            The two morphisms with codomain
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_1
            are inclusions, the other two are induced by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_0
            = succeed "-map" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
          %p
            T. Porter,
            = succeed "," do
              %em n-types of simplicial groups and crossed n-cubes
          %p
            which also contains the discussion of the generalisation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+n-cube"} crossed n-cubes
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#relation_to_groups
            Relation to
            = succeed "-groups" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi cat
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^2
          %p
            A crossed module
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo :
                  %mi M
                  %mo →
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu: M \to P
            determines a
            = succeed "-structure" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi cat
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semidirect+product"} semidirect product
            group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                    %mo ⋊
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M \rtimes P
            %em crossed module of crossed modules
            suggests that we should ask for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ⋊
                  %mi N
                  %mo →
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⋊
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L \rtimes N \to M \rtimes P
            to be a crossed module, so that there is an action which allows the
            %em big group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo ⋊
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋊
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi M
                  %mo ⋊
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = (L \rtimes N) \rtimes (M \rtimes P)
            to be a
            = succeed "-group." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi cat
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            becomes a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi cat
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^2
                \-
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cat-2-group"} group
            = succeed "-map" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            This equivalence between crossed squares and
            = succeed "-groups" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi cat
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            diagram, it looks as if you have to make appallingly detailed verifications of axioms. It is much easier to prove the corresponding diagram of
            = succeed "-groups" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi cat
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} cat^2
          %p
            This theme of using two equivalent categories, one for conjecture and proof, the other for calculation and application to traditional invariants, runs through the story of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+homotopy+van+Kampen+theorems"} higher homotopy van Kampen theorems
          %h3#relation_fo_2crossed_modules Relation fo 2-crossed modules
